    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8 sm:py-12">
      <header class="mb-10">
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-slate-900 font-heading mb-4 leading-tight">
          What Is a Good SAT Score? Score Ranges, Percentiles, and College Benchmarks for 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
          A data-driven guide to SAT score ranges, what percentile you fall in, the benchmarks that matter for different college tiers, and how to set a realistic target score for your applications.
        </p>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-4 text-xs text-slate-400">
          <time datetime="2026-02-16">Updated February 2026</time>
          <span>8 min read</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none space-y-8 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600">

        <p>
          "Is my SAT score good enough?" It's one of the most common questions students ask after getting their results — and the honest answer is <strong>it depends</strong>. A score that's competitive for one college may fall below the median at another. What counts as a "good" SAT score is entirely relative to your goals.
        </p>
        <p>
          This guide breaks down SAT score ranges by percentile, shows you the benchmarks for different college tiers, explains how section scores matter, and helps you figure out what target score makes sense for the schools on your list. If you've already taken a practice test, plug your raw scores into our <a href="/" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">SAT score calculator</a> to see where you stand.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 1 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="sat-score-scale">How the SAT Score Scale Works</h2>

        <p>
          The Digital SAT is scored on a scale of <strong>400 to 1600</strong>. That total is the sum of two section scores, each ranging from 200 to 800:
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Evidence-Based Reading and Writing (R&W):</strong> 200–800</li>
          <li><strong>Math:</strong> 200–800</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Your raw score — the number of questions you answered correctly — is converted to a scaled score through a process called <strong>equating</strong>, which adjusts for slight differences in difficulty between test forms. The Digital SAT also uses <a href="/how-digital-sat-is-scored" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">adaptive testing</a>: your performance on the first module of each section determines whether you get a harder or easier second module, which in turn affects your scoring ceiling.
        </p>

        <p>
          There's no penalty for wrong answers, so your raw score is simply the count of correct responses across both modules of a section.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 2 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="average-sat-score">What Is the Average SAT Score?</h2>

        <p>
          The national average SAT score is approximately <strong>1050</strong> out of 1600. That breaks down to roughly 530 in Reading & Writing and 520 in Math. This places the average test-taker near the <strong>50th percentile</strong> — meaning half of all students score above this mark and half score below.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-indigo-50 border border-indigo-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-indigo-800 !mt-0 !mb-2">What Does "Average" Mean for You?</h4>
          <p class="text-sm !mb-0">If you're scoring around 1050, you're in line with the national median. But "average" isn't the same as "good enough" for your goals. Students aiming for selective colleges typically need to score well above average — often 1200 or higher. Your target should be based on the admission profile of the schools you're applying to, not the national average.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 3 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="score-percentiles">SAT Score Percentile Chart</h2>

        <p>
          Percentiles tell you what percentage of test-takers you outperformed. A score at the 75th percentile means you scored higher than 75% of students. Here's how SAT total scores map to percentile rankings:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto !my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tl-lg">SAT Score</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Percentile</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tr-lg">What It Means</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1550–1600</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">99th+</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Top 1% — among the highest scores nationally</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1500–1540</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">98th–99th</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Competitive for the most selective schools</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1400–1490</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">94th–97th</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Strong for top-50 universities</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1300–1390</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">87th–93rd</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Above average; competitive for many selective schools</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1200–1290</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">75th–86th</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Good for most four-year colleges</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1100–1190</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">59th–74th</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Above average; solid for state universities</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">1000–1090</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">40th–58th</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Near the national average</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold rounded-bl-lg">Below 1000</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Below 40th</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 rounded-br-lg">Below average; additional prep recommended</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Want a more precise breakdown? Use our <a href="/sat-percentile-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">SAT percentile calculator</a> to see exactly where your score lands.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 4 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="college-benchmarks">SAT Score Benchmarks by College Tier</h2>

        <p>
          The most practical way to evaluate your SAT score is to compare it against the middle-50% range of admitted students at your target schools. The "middle 50%" represents the 25th to 75th percentile of enrolled students — scoring within or above this range means you're competitive.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">Ivy League and Top-10 Schools</h3>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto !my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tl-lg">School</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tr-lg">Middle 50% SAT Range</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">MIT</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1520–1580</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Harvard</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1490–1580</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Stanford</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1500–1570</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Princeton</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1500–1570</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Yale</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1470–1570</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold rounded-bl-lg">Columbia</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 rounded-br-lg">1490–1560</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          For Ivy League and top-10 schools, you generally need a <strong>1500+</strong> to be in the competitive range. Scoring above the 75th percentile of a school's range strengthens your application; scoring below the 25th doesn't disqualify you, but other parts of your application need to compensate.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">Top-25 to Top-50 Schools</h3>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto !my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tl-lg">School</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tr-lg">Middle 50% SAT Range</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">UCLA</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1290–1510</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">University of Michigan</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1340–1520</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">NYU</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1370–1530</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">University of Virginia</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1350–1510</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold rounded-bl-lg">Boston College</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 rounded-br-lg">1380–1520</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          For this tier, a score of <strong>1350–1500</strong> generally places you in the competitive range. These schools still have highly selective admissions, but the band is wider.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-8 !mb-3">State Flagships and Top-100 Schools</h3>

        <p>
          State flagship universities and schools ranked in the top 100 typically have middle-50% ranges between <strong>1150 and 1400</strong>. A score of 1200+ puts you in a strong position at most of these institutions. Examples include Penn State, University of Wisconsin, Ohio State, and Purdue.
        </p>

        <p>
          To see where your score stands against 120+ colleges, use our <a href="/" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">SAT score calculator</a> — it includes a college match tool that categorizes schools as safety, target, or reach based on your estimated score.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 5 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="section-scores">Do Section Scores Matter?</h2>

        <p>
          Yes. Your total score is what most people focus on, but section scores can matter for specific programs. Engineering and STEM applicants may face higher expectations for Math, while humanities programs may weigh R&W more heavily.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto !my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tl-lg">Section Score</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Approximate Percentile</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tr-lg">Assessment</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">750–800</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">99th+</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Exceptional — near-perfect performance</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">700–740</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">93rd–98th</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Excellent — competitive for top schools</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">650–690</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">82nd–92nd</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Strong — above average for most colleges</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">600–640</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">68th–81st</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Good — solid for state universities</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">500–590</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">30th–67th</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Average range</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold rounded-bl-lg">Below 500</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Below 30th</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 rounded-br-lg">Below average — focus improvement here</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-amber-800 !mt-0 !mb-2">Unbalanced Scores</h4>
          <p class="text-sm !mb-0">A total of 1400 could come from 750 R&W + 650 Math, or 650 R&W + 750 Math. While both add up the same, a significant imbalance might matter for your intended major. If you're applying to engineering programs, a stronger Math score carries more weight. Identify your weaker section and focus your prep there — that's where the biggest score gains are.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 6 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="good-score-by-goal">What Counts as "Good" Depends on Your Goal</h2>

        <p>
          There's no single number that qualifies as a universally good SAT score. Instead, define your target based on what you're trying to achieve:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto !my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tl-lg">Your Goal</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Target Score</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left rounded-tr-lg">Why</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Ivy League / Top 10</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1500+</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Places you at or above the 25th percentile of admitted students</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Top-50 university</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1350–1500</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Within the middle-50% range for most schools in this tier</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">State flagship / Top 100</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1200–1350</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Competitive for admission and merit scholarship consideration</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Merit scholarships</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1300+</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Many automatic scholarships kick in above this threshold</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold rounded-bl-lg">Beat the average</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">1100+</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 rounded-br-lg">Puts you above the national median</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          If you're not sure where to start, look up the middle-50% SAT range for every school on your list. Aim for the 75th percentile of your top-choice school — that gives you a comfortable margin.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 7 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="superscoring">Superscoring Can Raise Your Effective Score</h2>

        <p>
          Many colleges <a href="/can-you-superscore-digital-sat" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">superscore the SAT</a>, meaning they combine your highest R&W score and highest Math score from multiple test dates. If you scored 700 R&W and 650 Math on one sitting, then 660 R&W and 720 Math on another, your superscore would be <strong>700 + 720 = 1420</strong> — higher than either individual test.
        </p>

        <p>
          Superscoring means you don't need a perfect test day. You can focus on improving one section at a time across multiple sittings. Most top colleges — including all Ivy League schools — superscore.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 8 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="test-optional">Test-Optional Doesn't Mean Scores Don't Matter</h2>

        <p>
          Over 1,900 colleges have adopted test-optional policies as of 2026. This means you can <em>choose</em> whether to submit SAT scores. The strategic implication: <strong>submit your score if it falls within or above a school's middle-50% range</strong>. Withhold it if it falls below.
        </p>

        <p>
          At test-optional schools, submitted scores still carry weight in admissions decisions. Students who submit strong scores generally have higher admission rates than those who don't submit. A strong score also remains one of the fastest ways to qualify for automatic merit scholarships at many universities.
        </p>

        <!-- Section 9 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="how-to-set-target">How to Set Your Target Score</h2>

        <p>
          Follow these steps to set a realistic, data-driven target:
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
          <li>
            <strong>List your target schools.</strong> Include a mix of reach, target, and safety schools.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Look up each school's middle-50% SAT range.</strong> You can find this on each school's admissions page or Common Data Set.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Aim for the 75th percentile of your top-choice school.</strong> This gives you a strong position. For example, if your top choice has a range of 1350–1500, aim for 1500.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Take a diagnostic practice test.</strong> Use the College Board's Bluebook app to get a baseline score, then run it through our <a href="/sat-practice-test-score-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">practice test score calculator</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Calculate the gap.</strong> If your baseline is 1250 and your target is 1400, you need a 150-point improvement. Focus your study time on whichever section has more room to grow.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-200 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-emerald-800 !mt-0 !mb-2">Realistic Score Improvements</h4>
          <p class="text-sm !mb-0">With focused preparation, most students can improve <strong>100–200 points</strong> from their diagnostic baseline. Gains beyond 200 points are possible but require significantly more study time and targeted practice. Set an ambitious but achievable goal based on where you're starting from — not an arbitrary round number.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 10 -->
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 font-heading !mt-12 !mb-4" id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="space-y-6 !mt-6">
          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">What is a good SAT score?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">It depends on your goals. The national average is about 1050. A score of 1200+ is good for most four-year colleges (80th percentile), 1400+ is strong for top-50 schools (95th percentile), and 1500+ is competitive for Ivy League admissions (99th percentile).</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Is 1200 a good SAT score?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">Yes. A 1200 places you around the 80th percentile, meaning you scored higher than roughly four out of five test-takers. It's competitive for many state universities and solid four-year colleges, though it may be below the median at the most selective schools.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Is 1400 a good SAT score?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">A 1400 is an excellent score, placing you around the 95th percentile. It's competitive for most top-50 universities and within the middle-50% range at many highly selective schools.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">What SAT score do I need for the Ivy League?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">Ivy League middle-50% ranges generally fall between 1470 and 1580. A score of 1500+ makes you competitive, though admissions at these schools are holistic — grades, extracurriculars, essays, and recommendations all factor in.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Does a higher SAT score guarantee admission?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">No. Even a perfect 1600 doesn't guarantee admission at highly selective schools. SAT scores are one component of a holistic review that includes GPA, course rigor, extracurriculars, essays, and letters of recommendation.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Should I retake the SAT if my score is below my target?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm">If your score falls below the middle-50% range of your target schools, retaking is generally worth it — especially if you can identify specific areas to improve. Most students see gains of 100–200 points with dedicated study. Schools that <a href="/can-you-superscore-digital-sat" class="text-indigo-600 hover:underline">superscore</a> will use your best section scores from all sittings.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

